g is operator-driven through this variable. |
+| Host-split buffer IDENTITY and the plan-order dump (R51) | **Code, not an environment variable. No new environment variable was added, and that absence is the finding** — an ordering policy was the commission and it was REFUTED, not implemented. Always on, zero cost: `plan_begin` gained a `names` parameter (`ggml_backend_buffer_type_i`, `GGML_BACKEND_API_VERSION` 3 → 4), which `ggml-alloc.c` fills with each buffer's first tensor **that actually contributes bytes** (`alloc_range::first_alloc` — a range can open on a view, which would misname the whole buffer). Added 2026-09-15 (lane-240/R51). | Scope: two receipts and nothing else. (i) One stderr line beside the existing B7c line, ungated, only when a split is admitted: `host split placed N buffer(s) on host-visible memory: <name> <bytes> B, ...` (512-char cap, `(unnamed)` for a NULL **or empty** name — the F-175 156893184 B checkpoint buffers are unnamed and used to print as a bare size). (ii) Under the existing `GGML_VK_ALLOC_TRACE`, one line per plan entry naming the WHOLE batch, not just what moved. Names are advisory: `reserve_plan()` never reads them, so **placement is byte-for-byte what B7c decided** and outputs are bit-identical. | Nothing to operate. Read the receipt when a load splits; set `GGML_VK_ALLOC_TRACE=1` when you need to know whether a buffer you expected was even in the batch. | **Mechanism and diagnostic only — no placement change, no performance claim, and it exists because it REFUTED the lane's own commission.** The commission was to place the cheapest-to-read weight on the host mapping first (`token_embd.weight` is gathered one row per token; a layer weight is read in full every token). **That lever does not exist:** `token_embd.weight` is never in a Vulkan plan at any `-ngl`, because `llama-model.cpp:1519` pins `dev_input` to the CPU unconditionally. The 1.19 GB non-layer tensor that IS in the device batch is **`output.weight` (1191936000 B, entry 1 of 25)**, read in FULL for every token's